  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nStrong northern stream jet will settle across the northern Rockies \nwith low amplitude ridging over the Southeast leading to \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. More benign weather arrives towards the \nmid week as subtle heights build in and the early week disturbance \nlifts up into eastern Canada. Temperatures should recover to above \nnormal values, 60s to near 70F or 10 to 15F above normal for late \nOctober, for most locations across central and western Nebraska. \nDeep troughing will again dig south along the West Coast by late \nweek with a cutoff upper low taking shape. This should lead to more \namplified southwesterly flow locally with multiple shortwaves \ncrossing the central and southern Rockies in the days 5-10 \ntimeframe. As such, PoPs initialized from the model blend show a \nmarked increase into the weekend with even higher values early next \nweek. The latter of these align with the eventual progression of the \nlongwave trough through the Intermountain West towards the Plains. \nWhile the GEFS output is fairly noisy with greater uncertainty in \ntiming, the EPS is much more confident in seeing local impacts \nmaximized late Monday into early Tuesday (Day 8/9). Overall though, \nany impacts appear likely to favor primarily rain with any wintry \nconcerns limited to colder overnights. This is due to a lack of \nArtic air and, most significantly, no noteworthy Canadian high \npressure that is typical with our bigger winter events. Details are \nfar from certain so continue to check with subsequent forecasts for \nthe latest.",
